  "generation_note": {
    "why_no_llm_calls": "The corpus is 100% procedural: facts are integer functions of text bytes (plus catalog metadata for 2 of 24 categories), and falsehoods are splitmix64-derived. No LLM API was called at any stage. Reasons: (1) cost — an LLM-mediated corpus at this scale (millions of facts at the top end) would cost orders of magnitude more than the $0 procedural build; (2) determinism — every fact is byte-regenerable by anyone from the recorded Gutenberg URLs, with no model-version or sampling dependence; (3) auditability — truth is mechanically recomputable, so 'cheating' or teacher error is impossible by construction rather than by trust.",
    "what_we_lose": "Prose-mediated effects. The championship's planted falsehoods lived inside teacher prose (e.g. sol repeating 12 deliberate falsehoods word-perfect; SWE 'correcting' claims toward distractors), so it measured belief/claim dynamics, teacher fidelity, and learning-from-language. Here the falsehoods are synthetic numeric distractors with no semantic content, and the facts are surface statistics of text, not claims about the world. This corpus therefore measures numeric-fact learning under label noise at scale — it does NOT test linguistic generalization, teacher-quality effects, persuasion, or whether a learner can distinguish truth from fluent falsehood in prose. Those questions need an LLM-generated prose layer on top, which this corpus is designed to support but does not provide."
  },
